#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148235} :activity/id #uuid "8898632a-f568-581b-9193-aeb177e79e44" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049952 :topic/id #uuid "20d8e368-2cd0-5f3b-ab4b-9db7029241aa" :topic/math-academy-id 2512} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148738 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a tank is filling up with water, and the rate at which water flows into the tank is given by\n\n$$\nr(t) = 600e^{-3t},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per hour, and $t > 0$ is the time (in hours) since the water flow started. \n\nSuppose we want to know how much water enters the tank between $1$ and $2$ hours. \n\nLet's take note of the following:\n\n- We're interested in the amount of water that enters the tank between $t=1$ and $t=2.$ In other words, we want the **net change in volume** between these times. We're *not* finding the total volume of water in the tank.\n\n- We're given the **rate of change of volume** $r(t).$\n\nTo find the net change in volume, we integrate $r(t)$ between the two limits. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}600{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={-\\frac{600}{3}{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={-200{e}^{-3t}|}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =-200({e}^{-6}-{e}^{-3}) \\\\  & =200({e}^{-3}-{e}^{-6}) \\\\  & =200{e}^{-6}({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60a3bf2a-7142-575f-8381-dfb70827c7c2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2744 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "62e34955-26a4-56d7-b473-2f2540475511" :step/math-academy-id 13518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147333 :question/id #uuid "a3030491-423d-5633-8208-b373bfee3b06" :question/math-academy-id "e-3892"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19641aa-8e39-5348-8565-9f4ce030d18f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050200}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319354} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357465}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Net Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "98d275b2-d6f2-53c5-b4d7-cae25b562f1a" :step/math-academy-id 13519 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148739 :tutorial/content "If we're interested to find the **total number** or **total amount** of some quantity, we should use the fundamental theorem of calculus.\n\nLet's go back to the earlier example with the shopping mall (Example 1). Suppose that there are $2\\,000$ people inside the mall at the moment that it opened. How many people in total are inside the mall after $2$ hours?\n\nWe already know that the **rate of change** in the number of people is $R(t).$ Therefore, the **total number of people** in the mall at time $t$, which we'll call $T(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important**: You'll notice that we changed the variable of integration from $t$ to $x.$ This is to ensure that $T$ comes out as a function of $t,$ and also so we don't get confused. When we have a definite integral, it does not matter which variable we use!\n\nTo work out how many people are inside the mall at $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\,000+{{{\\int }_{0}^{2}r(x)-25\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{850} \\\\  & =2\\,000+850 \\\\  & =2\\,850\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02950c6c-bf43-5c34-885c-8da2caa06fbf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2745 :tutorial/title "Finding Total Amounts Given Rates of Change"} :step/id #uuid "2755fa08-44ef-5ef5-b8c8-6470c68e446a" :step/math-academy-id 13520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147334 :question/id #uuid "d007e9ad-3fd3-5ae2-864a-ef89ea01785c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3893"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f692eea3-1a29-5067-86dd-16a56be40703" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332971} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403092}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Total Change of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "abe470e8-0b76-56c6-9c15-a1d10e391c4b" :step/math-academy-id 13521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148239}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148239 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147399 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147335 :question/id #uuid "74125c40-ecdd-5453-9c49-13567fb4621c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3798ec35-19ef-559f-b8f8-cb0a0a03157b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048498}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319359} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400895}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Global Minimum or Maximum of a Function Given Its Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "5d3cad10-c38a-51fd-b011-83786859f767" :step/math-academy-id 13522}] :activity/title "Integrating Rates of Change" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147695}]}
